e to the million  veteran program the million veteran  programs was initiated in 2011  by the va as a as a way to  to explore uh how genes lifestyles and  exposure affect health  and illness we have we collect  as part of the million veteran program  we collect biospecimens that are then  linked to the ehr  and self-reported and other health data  to give you a glance of what we have  done so far the mvp has collected over  828 000  samples we we we collect uh we  the collection sites are all across the  the united states  uh we have roughly about 60 61 rowing  rowing collection sites um that that  moves from  from station to station um  depending on on the interest shown by  the veterans to the mailers that we sent  about the mvp program  so to give you an overview of the mvp  data universe  from the mvp participants we collect  self-reported surveys  we essentially have two types of surveys  that we send out  one is a short five pager that gives  us questions about their health  in general and then we send out a much  longer 15 page surveys that that is more  of a lifestyle survey  we collect uh 10 ml blood as a biosample  from each of the participants when they  when they um when they sign their hipaa  and consent  and from these biosamples we generate  multiple molecular  data that that that can then be um  integrated with clinical data that we  get from the emr  from the va the cdw the corporate data  warehouse that sort of abstracts all the  all the clinical data from the emr  system so this provides  a an opportunity to integrate  longitudinal  clinical data with their molecular data  we also get data from other sources  including medicare  various disease registries national debt  index etc  so how do we do bio banking in mvp  the recruitment to mvp is basically an  all-comers  the inclusion criteria criteria is that  you have to be a veteran  and user of the va healthcare system and  there is no  no exclusion criteria so to speak of  we collect um 10 ml of blood in an edta  vacutainer at the  time of recruitment and these blood  samples are shipped overnight on ice  packs to the central repository in  boston most samples arrive  in 24 to 48 hours accessioning is done  into the lab's limb system without any  clinical identifiers  we save multiple aliquots of plasma  buffy codes and  an aliquot of of the buffy coat is then  made into dna which is then sent out for  uh molecular molecular analysis  we take care not to to have any sorting  biases and that's why  not having any clinical identifiers in  the lymph system  um helps because this is not we are not  collecting  it's not a case control study or we are  not collecting any disease specific so  it's an all comers  and and for the labs it is a sample  and that's it so to start to look at the  sort of the genomic um  analysis we first developed an mvp  genotyping array we developed this with  the help of  um affymetrix or thermofisher  uh on the axiom platform we use the  um the axiom biobank as sort of the  backbone onto which we added about 100k  custom  snips mainly the snips that are  clinically relevant over the next  few slides um i'll just go through very  briefly you know the kind of  design decisions that we took when we  built the mvp  custom array as you can see the backbone  has the  the axiom biobank content which includes  pharmacogenics  genetics exome grid loss of function g  was  um coverage um snips etc  on the custom content we we included a  lot of  a lot of clinically relevant snips um  including you know  sort of disease diagnostic or prognostic  disease relevant snips  including ras we also uh include a lot  of  hra hla and kir region snips with the  idea of being able to to see if we can  find map these regions um  better we also used uh increased sort of  the  you know have tax names that allows us  to increase the  the imputation quality in different  ethnicities  in terms of the clinical relevance of  the mvp  array this is an example where we are  looking at  the 10 top 10 acmg genes and how  the number of snips that have been used  on the microarray  to cover them we pretty much have  all the the entire acmg list is probed  on this array  we also um you as i said earlier that we  we have been um using typing a lot of  snips that are sort of  important for disease groups and here's  a list of  examples of the research areas and the  diseases that  that have been probed by these markers  so this is an overview of the sample and  data processing  workflow in mvp so we start with sample  collection  we do the data extraction in our  biorepository and we send it out for  genotyping  since mvp is continuing to  enroll participants and the analysis is  done over a longer period of time we pay  particular attention to how batching is  done  so we make sure that the the samples  that are collected together  are acid together um on on  on the axiom platform um we with  thermofisher we have developed  a a lot of um advanced  uh uh qc uh methods including  plate normalizations uh sample quality  control  sample and marker quality controls and  also  methods to sort of minimize batch  effects  we do multiple data quality control  assessments  and then we also generate sort of  secondary data in terms of relatedness  genetic ancestry  etc and we validate the sample uh the  data  before using you know height as a as an  example of ajiwas  before before making the data available  for analysis  as i mentioned earlier the  the mvp array has a lot of low frequency  alleles  because they are clinically significant  markers  as you can see on the on the left the  missingness increases  the different bucket buckets of  missingness on the x-axis you can see as  the missingness increases  as we have more rare alleles  on the right you can look at the um what  i'm showing you  is sort of the minor allele discardance  um as you can see  uh for the common alleles one percent  are above  um we we have the the cumulative  fraction of markers  um in in that bucket um the the allele  discordance is is very very low  so even with uh for the allele frequency  bins of less than 0.001  um we have more than um eighty percent  uh concordance  another um quality metrics that we look  at is  um duplicate sample concordance um as  part of our  of our analysis plan  we send about five percent of the  samples back to the vendors  without um telling them that they that  these are duplicate samples  so on the left you can see the the  absolute concordance between those  duplicate samples  within the vendors and and across  vendors it's  pretty close to 100 and above 99  and on the right you can look at the  minor allele discordance  again within between vendors and within  vendors the  myeloma discordance rate is is very low  one of the things that we did is that we  compared the  allele frequency of of mvp as a whole  first to the thousand genomes so in this  you can see the different mile early  frequency bins  and the mvp is is a gray bar  and the um the thousand genome ancestry  populations are the different colored  bars  and as you can see um mvp has  a um you know the mvp allele frequency  is spread across across  the entire data set what we have  noticed is that if we split the mvp into  the ancestry  subsets for example the allele frequency  of mvp seems to be  different from the thousand genome  indicating that there is a lot of  diversity in in the mvp population  we also have done a lot of work on  identifying  related individuals and to make sure  that  when the data is used for geos we get  good quality  you know gos analysis results so we have  used  the kinship analysis to to differentiate  between  for example parents offsprings full  siblings and first cousins  and we are also able to pick up  [Music]  duplicates or twins in that process  we have also looked at runs of  homozygosity  um as you know um the  the bottleneck populations should have  longer runs of homo  runs of homozygosity while the admix  population  should have shorter runs and we can see  that the  the range that we have in mvp indicating  that you know we have  um you know populations across the  spectrum  in terms of um admixture  here's what i'm showing you is  the projection pc where we  we projected that the mvp which is in  grey  on to the onto the thousand thousand  genome population with uh pc one and two  on the left  being shown on the left um as you can  see that  um yes mvp does have the the caucasians  and the  yri and and the asian cluster  but there is a a pretty good spread of  mvp samples  between those clusters indicating again  the diversity  that we have in mvp and on the right is  just  another way of showing you the same  thing where grace tell the mvp  um another way of looking at genetic  diversity in the mvp  sample about 70 of the mvp samples are  clustered with the gbr population um for  example  but you can see that there is a large  number of population that cluster with  yri  and other various sort of ad mixed  populations so to speak  so this gives us an ability to have  reasonably large numbers of each of  those subsets of  ancestries which should should really  help us  um in in doing gus's within those  subsets  rather than doing across all the genetic  subs  ancestry subsets so in conclusion  um mvp is is very large we have more  than 800 000  already and we are still uh continuing  to recruit  and as i showed you it is very diverse  cohort and should enable genetic  analysis across stratified populations  meaning that you know even the the  minority populations we should have  enough  in number to be able to do statistically  relevant  uh relevant conclusions from g was  analysis for example  we have very rich and longitudinal  clinical data that is associated  with these samples and which allows us  to do very fine phenotyping  in terms of disease and subsets of  diseases  mvp data in general is of very high  quality  and overall what i want to  as a take home what i want to say is  that you know  mvp cohort will allow us to increase the  scale of non-european populations um  upon which genetic analysis has been  done this should really  allow for building population subsets  specific prs  and even disease-specific prs because  you know  we we have enough number the end should  should allow us to  do meaningful um statistical conclusions  and with that i want to sort of  acknowledge um all the peoples involved  in it  um especially the mvp participants for  their altruistic  donation of samples and their data to  the mvp program  thank you everyone

